---
import {Code} from "astro:components";
import ReferencePage from "../layouts/ReferencePage.astro";
import {ERROR_FACTS,SUBSCRIPTION_STATE_FACTS,MAP_HANDLER_FAILURE} from "../release-facts.ts";
import handlerSource from "../snippets/recovery.ts?raw";
import {excerpt} from "../snippets/excerpt.ts";
import {RELEASE} from "../site.ts";
import {AUTOMATIC_ADVANCE_LIMIT,DISPOSITION_LIFECYCLE,FAILURE_SOURCES,INSTALLED_VALIDATOR_ON_FAILURE_HANDLING,NOT_OFFERED,OPERATOR_TABLE,POLICIES,POLICY_MATRIX,REDRIVE_OUTCOMES} from "../failure-handling.ts";
const accepted=INSTALLED_VALIDATOR_ON_FAILURE_HANDLING.valid;
---
<ReferencePage title="When it breaks" description="Follow the state first, then the error. Some failures affect one view; others pause the shared source.">
<section><h2>Explore a view’s state</h2><p>This is an explanatory diagram, not a connection to the demo. Select a state to see what it means.</p><div class="reference-card"><label for="state-picker">Subscription state</label><select id="state-picker">{Object.keys(SUBSCRIPTION_STATE_FACTS).map(state=><option value={state}>{state}</option>)}</select><p aria-label="Typical recovery path">authorizing → synchronizing → live ⇄ stale → resync-required<br/>A denied or broken subscription → failed. Unsubscribe → closed.</p>{Object.entries(SUBSCRIPTION_STATE_FACTS).map(([state,fact],i)=><div data-state-detail={state} hidden={i!==0}><h3>{state}</h3><p>{fact.description}</p></div>)}<span class="sr-only" role="status" id="state-announcement"></span></div></section>
<section><h2>One failure, two perspectives</h2><p>{MAP_HANDLER_FAILURE.summary}</p><div class="reference-grid"><div class="reference-card"><h3>Already-open browser view</h3><p><code>stale / SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE</code></p><p>The view keeps its last data, visibly stale. A source pause is a state event; it does not invoke the subscription’s error listener.</p></div><div class="reference-card"><h3>Development gateway trace</h3><p><code>map / failed / HANDLER_FAILED</code></p><p>The trace identifies the operator’s cause without exposing payloads. The production gateway has no trace endpoint.</p></div></div><p>{MAP_HANDLER_FAILURE.recovery}</p><p>A subscription that starts synchronizing while the source is already paused can receive the source’s specific reason. The normalization above describes an already-open view becoming stale.</p><p>Pausing is the default response to a bad record. In {RELEASE} you can opt into more for two failure classes: see <a href="#source-failure-policies">the source-failure policies</a> below.</p></section>
<section><h2>Handle state and errors separately</h2><Code code={excerpt(handlerSource)} lang="ts" theme="night-owl" /><p>The sample is type-checked against the pinned package. A rejected wait does not necessarily stop the subscription underneath it.</p></section>
<section><h2>Error reference</h2><p>The retryable flag below is the package default. Read the situation and any override: the flag alone does not describe the complete recovery policy.</p>{Object.entries(ERROR_FACTS).map(([code,fact])=><details><summary>{code} — {fact.publicMessage}</summary><p>{fact.meaning}</p><p><strong>Default retryable:</strong> {String(fact.defaultRetryable)}. {fact.retryableNotes}</p><ul>{fact.situations.map(s=><li>{s}</li>)}</ul><p><strong>Browser SDK:</strong> {fact.visibility.browserSdk}</p><p><strong>Gateway trace:</strong> {fact.visibility.gatewayTrace}</p><p><strong>Operator log:</strong> {fact.visibility.operatorLog}</p><p><a href={fact.sources[0]}>Pinned release reference ↗</a></p></details>)}</section>
<section id="source-failure-policies" aria-labelledby="policies-title" data-failure-policies>
<h2 id="policies-title">Source-failure policies in streamotter@{RELEASE}</h2>
<p class="notice"><strong>Opt-in.</strong> Everything above is what happens without a <code>failureHandling</code> section, and it stays the default: a bad record pauses its source. {RELEASE} adds policies you can turn on per source and per failure class; the installed validator accepts a <code>failureHandling</code> section{accepted && <> (this demo's own configuration passes with a <code>quarantine-hold</code> policy added)</>}. The <a href={FAILURE_SOURCES.guide}>Handle bad records guide ↗</a> is the runbook. This section describes the library; what this demo runs is at the end.</p>
<p>A class with no policy pauses. The policies:</p>
<dl class="policy-terms">{Object.entries(POLICIES).map(([name,text])=><><dt><code>{name}</code></dt><dd>{text}</dd></>)}</dl>
<p>There is no {NOT_OFFERED.map((name,i)=><>{i>0&&(i===NOT_OFFERED.length-1?", or ":", ")}<code>{name}</code></>)} option. Automatic continuation stops after {AUTOMATIC_ADVANCE_LIMIT.incidents} distinct incidents in {AUTOMATIC_ADVANCE_LIMIT.windowMs/1000} seconds on one source, by default; the next one is held until an operator reopens the circuit after a repair. A record with several problems takes the most serious class, so a quarantine policy never moves past a record that also has a mapping or integrity problem.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="matrix-title"><table><caption id="matrix-title">Failure-policy matrix</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Failure</th><th scope="col">Failure class</th><th scope="col">With no policy</th><th scope="col">The most a policy may do</th></tr></thead><tbody>{POLICY_MATRIX.map(row=><tr><th scope="row">{row.failure}</th><td>{row.classes.length?row.classes.map((name,i)=><>{i>0&&", "}<code>{name}</code></>):"Not a record failure"}</td><td>{row.byDefault}</td><td>{row.optIn}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p class="source-line"><a href={FAILURE_SOURCES.policy}>Guide §3: choose a policy ↗</a> · <a href={FAILURE_SOURCES.barrier}>ADR-15B: failure classes ↗</a></p>
<h3 id="record-disposition">Record disposition, step by step</h3>
<p>One failing record under a quarantine policy. Each step is recorded separately, and none of them stands in for another.</p>
<ol class="lifecycle">{DISPOSITION_LIFECYCLE.map(stage=><li><h4>{stage.name}</h4><p>{stage.what}</p><p><strong>Doesn't mean:</strong> {stage.notProof}</p><p class="source-line">Terms: {stage.terms.map((term,i)=><>{i>0&&", "}<code>{term}</code></>)} · <a href={stage.source}>Source ↗</a></p></li>)}</ol>
<h3>What the browser sees</h3>
<p>Subscription states don't change. <code>held</code>, <code>quarantine-unknown</code>, <code>advance-confirmed</code>, and the rest are incident facts for operators, not SDK states. An affected view goes stale with <code>SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE</code>, as without a policy, and becomes live again only through a fresh snapshot that satisfies the recovery boundary. There is no “live with gaps” state.</p>
<h3>Operator actions on one incident</h3>
<p>Commands of the local <code>streamotter</code> CLI, which reaches the gateway through its operator socket on the gateway's own machine. They are never browser controls, and visitors to this site can't reach them. Every action that changes state names the revision it expects; there is no force, wildcard, or bulk form.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Operator actions"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Command</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">Changes state</th></tr></thead><tbody>{OPERATOR_TABLE.map(action=><tr><th scope="row"><code>streamotter {action.command}</code></th><td>{action.what}</td><td>{action.mutates?"Yes":"No"}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p>A redrive records one of four outcomes. None of them means a business action completed or that every browser rendered the result.</p>
<dl class="policy-terms">{Object.entries(REDRIVE_OUTCOMES).map(([name,text])=><><dt><code>{name}</code></dt><dd>{text}</dd></>)}</dl>
<p class="source-line"><a href={FAILURE_SOURCES.operate}>Guide §5.2: act ↗</a> · <a href={FAILURE_SOURCES.operator}>V1.1 API §6: operator service ↗</a> · <a href={FAILURE_SOURCES.redrive}>ADR-15C ↗</a></p>
<h3>What this demo runs</h3>
<p>The live creek and the walkthrough configure no failure policies. The Failure Lab's <a href="/lab/#source-failures">Source failures track</a> lists exercises built on these policies; it asks the demo's backend which of them its benches can run, and gives the reason for any it can't.</p>
</section>
</ReferencePage>
<script>
const picker=document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>("#state-picker")!;
picker.addEventListener("change",()=>{for(const node of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-state-detail]"))node.hidden=node.dataset["stateDetail"]!==picker.value;document.querySelector("#state-announcement")!.textContent=`Showing ${picker.value}.`;});
</script>
<style>
.policy-terms { display:grid; grid-template-columns:max-content minmax(0,1fr); gap:10px 18px; margin-block:16px; }
.policy-terms dd { margin:0; }
caption { text-align:left; font-weight:700; padding-bottom:10px; }
.lifecycle { display:grid; gap:14px; padding-inline-start:24px; }
.lifecycle li { padding:16px 18px; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:var(--radius); background:var(--surface); }
.lifecycle h4 { margin:0 0 6px; }
.source-line { font-size:.88rem; color:var(--ink-2); }
[data-failure-policies] h3 { margin-top:32px; }
@media (max-width:560px) { .policy-terms { grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr); } .policy-terms dd { margin-bottom:8px; } }
</style>
